  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1134 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nSunday through Friday:\n\nSfc high pressure and upper level ridging is the name of the\ngame for much of the extended. With this being the dominate\npattern through midweek, there wont be much of a change. Dry\nconditions are expected with light to moderate winds. The sfc\nhigh will be overhead Sunday into Sunday night and move east of\nWisconsin by Monday. Southwest winds will bring a warming trend\nto the Great Lakes Region through the week. Models continue to\nhit at a potential for 60s again Friday. \n\nThe next chance for rain looks to return Thursday. A trough is\nexpected to move from the Rockies northeast into the Northern \nPlains and continue into Canada. The strength of the trough\nvaries a bit between the GFS, CAN, and Euro. There are also\nslight variances between the placement and timing. Overall the\nconsensus seems to be that the sfc low will pass to the north\nand drag a cold front across the state. With a sfc high to the\nsouth there may be limited moisture moving northward as well.\nGiven the variability and low moisture returns confidence on \nrain is still fairly low which is why POPs remain low around \n10-20%. For those hoping for rain, long range guidance does hint\nat a stronger trough moving through the western CONUS and into \nthe central CONUS for Friday into Saturday so rain chances may \npersist. \n\nPatterson",
